"agent:T3","usage":{"input_tokens":350165,"output_tokens":3494,"requests":9}}],"version":1},"findings":[{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Add an acceptance oracle (and/or a runtime assertion) that pins `mcp_run_new`'s env-var + bind-mount + restart/stop-timeout set to the compose `mcp:` service, so a drift between the hand-mirrored `docker run` flags and the compose block fails a test rather than silently shipping a mis-configured container that still passes the config-independent /health check.","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"block","decision_margin":0.3,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan: 'every REBAR_MCP_* + REBAR_OPCERT_* var with the SAME literal defaults compose's ${VAR:-default} interpolates (docker run does not interpolate) … a comment marks these MIRRORING compose mcp:'","src/rebar/_mcp_health.py register_health_route: '/health … OUTSIDE the auth and transport-security middleware — an unauthenticated probe gets 200', so a config-drifted container still health-checks green","docker-compose.yml mcp: sets REBAR_MCP_HTTP_ALLOWED_HOSTS/ORIGINS, REBAR_MCP_AUTH_* and two :ro mounts via ${VAR:-default} interpolation that docker run will not reproduce","AC list names cutover/liveness/memory/health oracles but no env-parity oracle between mcp_run_new and the compose mcp: service"],"finding":"The hand-mirrored `docker run -d` flag set in `mcp_run_new` (every REBAR_MCP_*/REBAR_OPCERT_* env var, the two :ro bind-mounts, --restart always, --stop-timeout) can silently DRIFT from the compose `mcp:` service, and the /health gate cannot catch such drift because /health is unauthenticated and config-independent (register_health_route mounts it OUTSIDE auth/transport-security middleware in src/rebar/_mcp_health.py). A wrong/missing REBAR_MCP_HTTP_ALLOWED_HOSTS or REBAR_MCP_AUTH_* would still return /health 200, flip the upstream, and ship a mis-configured or fail-open MCP endpoint. The plan relies only on a MIRRORING comment; no acceptance oracle pins the docker run env/mount set to the compose mcp: block.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f82a09785f6e93a0b","impact":1.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Run mechanism + the original compose container (bootstrap); Acceptance Criteria","norm_id":"n1538652431e3a504","priority":0.9,"reason":"high-priority+criterion-opted-in","recalled":false,"scenarios":["esok edits the compose mcp: env (e.g. adds/renames an allowed-host or auth var) but the autodeploy.sh mcp_run_new mirror is not updated; the next mcp deploy ships a container missing that var, passes /health, flips the upstream, and serves /mcp mis-configured (potentially fail-open on the DNS-rebinding guard)"],"severity":"critical","suggested_fix":"Either (a) add a test that parses infra/compose/docker-compose.yml's mcp: environment/volumes and asserts every key/mount appears in mcp_run_new with the same literal default; or (b) reconsider generating the parallel container from a compose override/second project (COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME + a port-override file) so compose's own ${VAR:-default} interpolation is the single source of truth and no hand-mirroring can drift — the plan rejects `docker compose up mcp` for the 8091 pin but does not address this override-project alternative.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.9,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"yes","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"yes","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"yes","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"yes","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"missing_oracle","blast_radius":"module","debt_impact":"medium","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"uncertifiable_outcome","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"medium","prod_impact":"high","reversibility":"moderate","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.7,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] State a rollback step that resets /etc/nginx/mcp-upstream.conf back to the 8091 seed (compose-original) as part of reverting the mcp additions.","cohort":["COH","E1","E2","E6","F1","T2","T4","T5a","T5e","T7"],"criteria":["T4"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.37,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["'Rollback = revert the additions.'","'seed include→8091' but deploys flip the include to 8092/8093","'on reboot compose recreates compose-mcp-1 with NO collision'"],"finding":"Rollback ('revert the additions') does not address the deployed nginx include state: after a deploy the mcp-upstream.conf points at a blue-green port (8092/8093), but reverting autodeploy.sh leaves no process managing that container and the include may point at a port no longer served after reboot (compose recreates compose-mcp-1 on 8091, not 8092/8093).","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f72947419e99ef14c","impact":0.33,"is_primary":null,"location":"Compat / rollback section","norm_id":"n3de771f33a7a57fd","priority":0.33,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["Operator reverts the autodeploy additions after an mcp deploy has flipped the upstream to 8092; on next reboot compose brings up compose-mcp-1 on 8091 but the include still names 8092 → /mcp/ 502s."],"severity":"minor","suggested_fix":"Add to Compat/rollback: 'Revert also rewrites mcp-upstream.conf to `server 127.0.0.1:8091;` + nginx -s reload so routing returns to the compose-original container.'","tier":"LLM","validity":1.0,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committe